<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Boisbelle</h1><p class="st-meta">1597 CE – 1766 CE (approx.) · capital Henrichemont</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A sovereign enclave in Berry, exempt from royal taxes, where Henry IV's minister Sully built the planned town of Henrichemont.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Boisbelle was a lordship in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berry-province">Berry</a> whose holders had long claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, a privilege tolerated by the French crown. In 1597 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilien-de-bethune-duke-of-sully">Maximilien de Béthune</a>, later duke of Sully, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV</a>'s chief minister, bought it and confirmed its status as a principality.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilien-de-bethune-duke-of-sully">Sully</a> founded the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henrichemont">Henrichemont</a> in 1608, named after the king, on a regular grid around a central square, hoping to attract Protestant settlers and artisans. Its inhabitants paid no royal taxes, which drew some settlers, though the town never grew as planned. The Béthune family ruled the enclave for over 150 years.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1766 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilien-de-bethune-duke-of-sully">Duke of Sully's</a> descendants sold the principality to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a>, ending its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, and it became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1597 CE – 1766 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">France and the Low Co